<p>Hi! Thanks for posting to the site!</p>
<p>After reading it, I have decided on no-vote for now, as I've identified some tone errors and word choice issues.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Note&nbsp;1</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Note&nbsp;1</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Beyond the assigned research and containment staff chosen, no other personnel are to enter this area of exclusion. Any individuals or personnel that do are to be detained and brought in for questioning. Detained individuals are to undergo therapeutic treatment and amnesticized from ever encountering SCP-4622-1.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This section reads a little bit weird, specifically the &quot;&#8230;and amnesticized from ever encountering&#8230;&quot;. People usually aren't &quot;amnesticized <em>from</em>&quot; anything. It might be easier to say:</p>
<p>Suggestion:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>No other personnel are authorized to enter the exclusion zone. Violators are to be detained for questioning then amnesticized. Psychotherapy is then to be administered, as needed.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A lot of clinical tone comes from being as clear and concise as possible i.e. use as few words to say what you have to say.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Note&nbsp;2</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Note&nbsp;2</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Description: SCP-4622 is an unmarked and open grave in front of a tree near the peak of a hill located in the Chugach Mountains of Alaska. A tombstone is affixed to the grave but bears no name or identity of the figure lying within.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Even though it isn't, the first sentence here reads reads like a run-on.<br />
&quot;An unmarked and open grave&#8230;<br />
<strong>IN FRONT OF</strong> a tree&#8230;.<br />
<strong>NEAR</strong> the peak&#8230;<br />
<strong>OF A</strong> hill&#8230;<br />
<strong>LOCATED IN</strong> the Chugach Mountains<br />
<strong>OF</strong> Alaska.&quot;</p>
<p>Suggestion:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Description: SCP-4622 is the designation for an anomalous open gravesite in the Chugach Mountains of Alaska. A tombstone is present, but bears no name or other markings.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Because it's so early in the article, you want to have things to draw the reader in. If you do all the work for them, they might not like it.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Note&nbsp;3</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Note&nbsp;3</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The figure, hereafter referred to as SCP-4622-1, is a corpse in an advanced state of decomposition that is heavily distorted and warped beyond human perception. Further observation of SCP-4622-1 has been known to cause mild eyestrain, headaches, and nosebleeds in the observing individual. SCP-4622-1 carries another anomalous effect: observers will perceive the figure in the grave as themselves and will attempt to fill it with soil from SCP-4622-2.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A segment that bothers me a lot is &quot;heavily distorted and warped beyond human perception.&quot; Distorted in what way? Has the body physically changed? What does &quot;beyond human perception&quot; mean? Can we not perceive it? Imagine a researcher who isn't tasked to this anomaly reading this report; I don't think they'd get very much out that sentence.</p>
<p>We also really don't need to designate every little thing as an SCP-4622-#, even if those things are anomalous. If they're never seprable from the actual anomaly (the gravesite), designating them with their own numbers just serves to clutter the article. That said, the body might still deserve it's own designation, but the pile of soil definitely does not.</p>
<p>Suggestion:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>An unidentified human corpse, designated SCP-4622-1, is present within the grave. The body is in an advanced state of decomposition. When viewed by an observer SCP-4622-1 exhibits mild cognitohazardous effects, inducing eyestrain, headaches, and nosebleeds. This is secondary to its primary anomalous effect: observers will perceive the body as their own and attempt to fill the grave using the surrounding soil.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Note&nbsp;4</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Note&nbsp;4</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-4622-2 is a mound of soil with a shovel affixed to it, located to the right, beside the grave. SCP-4622-2 instantly replenishes any of its mass lost when not observed. Likewise, any attempt to fill SCP-4622 will be unsuccessful as no matter how much soil has been shoveled into the grave, SCP-4622 will always remain unchanged.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Following what was said above in Note 3, the soil mound doesn't need its own designation.</p>
<p>Suggestion:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When unobserved, any progress made on filling the grave will be anomalously reverted back to its undisturbed state.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>It's getting late and I'm sorry I do not have notes for you regarding the dialogue portion.</p>
<p>Thank you again for posting. I hope this feedback is more helpful than not.</p>

							</div>

								
					
